WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.180 --> 00:00:07.379
To the human eye there appears to be a
glowing three-dimensional butterfly

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:18.800
moving around inside this black box. In
fact all it is, is a tiny polystyrene bead.

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:20.800
Wow, that's really small.

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:24.360
Yes, it's like a one millimeter, two millimeter bead.

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:31.360
It's suspended and we can move it so
fast to create a 3D shape.

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:38.360
This is a volumetric display. It's an image that
exists in real 3D space and this latest

00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:43.100
development in the field is unique in
that it uses sound waves not only to

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:48.480
create the image but also to add audio
and tactile sensations.

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:56.280
Oh yep you can feel, it's like a really, oh like a kind
of gentle buzz or breeze.

00:00:56.280 --> 00:01:01.580
My name's Lizzie Gibney and I've come to the University
of Sussex in the UK to see that display

00:01:01.580 --> 00:01:06.540
for myself. The most surprising thing
about it is that it doesn't look like a

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:10.660
flat projection. You can see that it's
really floating there in three

00:01:10.660 --> 00:01:15.060
dimensions and in person the image
doesn't flicker like it does on camera.

00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:19.080
People don't believe it and then there's
this magical moment where they go

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:20.700
like "wow that's real"

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:24.420
The display works by a process
called acoustic levitation.

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:29.640
In the walls of this box are arrays of speakers
producing sound waves.

00:01:29.660 --> 00:01:37.920
So by using the ultrasound, creating a standing wave and this small tiny small bead.

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:40.880
The speaker's create areas of high pressure

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:45.660
that can trap a polystyrene
bead and suspend it in midair. Moving the

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:50.790
bead around and shining a light on it
creates a glowing moving dot. Move that

00:01:50.790 --> 00:01:54.700
dot around fast enough, and you can
create shapes in the air.

00:01:54.700 --> 00:02:00.500
What we've created is an acoustophoretic
volumetric display. So we basically use

00:02:00.509 --> 00:02:06.340
sound to trap a lightweight object in free space and

00:02:06.340 --> 00:02:11.860
we can update its position at very high
update rates. So fast that persistence of

00:02:11.860 --> 00:02:14.700
vision happens and you think it's a full
object

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:16.720
The team was inspired by research

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:21.489
published last year from Brigham Young
University the American group had

00:02:21.489 --> 00:02:25.870
successfully created a volumetric
display by steering tiny particles of

00:02:25.870 --> 00:02:28.660
cellulose using laser light.

00:02:28.660 --> 00:02:30.819
They were trapping a particle with lasers and

00:02:30.819 --> 00:02:36.730
doing something very similar and for us,
to be honest, we were not even thinking

00:02:36.730 --> 00:02:41.400
that that could be a possibility you
know, but we looked at each other and said like

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:46.500
"You think we can do that?" or like "I don't know let's try"

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:48.370
The team at the University of Sussex has

00:02:48.370 --> 00:02:52.239
been working on using sound waves to
move small objects or create sensations

00:02:52.239 --> 00:02:58.060
for a few years. But the big challenge in
creating a volumetric display was to get

00:02:58.060 --> 00:03:02.410
the beads moving fast enough that it
appears as a single non moving image.

00:03:02.410 --> 00:03:09.300
To see as a image for human eyes the
whole path for example if you want

00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:17.960
to create a circle, this circle should be
scanned in 100 milliseconds, something like that.

00:03:17.960 --> 00:03:25.120
So the beads should be moved so fast to create this path in 100 milliseconds.

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:27.250
But actually that's not the
end of it right, so because we are

00:03:27.250 --> 00:03:34.140
updating at that speed we can also
create audio and tactile feedback so the

00:03:34.150 --> 00:03:39.819
object is singing as it's moving
and at the same time if you bring your

00:03:39.820 --> 00:03:42.660
hand close to it you will get a
sensation of touch.

00:03:42.700 --> 00:03:47.500
The researchers can use the same waves of pressure that control the bead to create ripples in

00:03:47.500 --> 00:03:52.450
the air. Fluttering on a finger to give a
tactile experience that's something that

00:03:52.450 --> 00:03:55.220
no other kind of volumetric display can
do.

00:03:55.220 --> 00:03:58.630
We create that focusing point to

00:03:58.630 --> 00:04:04.000
another place for tactile feedback so
it's like a levitation, tactile,

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:07.940
levitation, tactile, so we can switch so
quickly.

00:04:07.940 --> 00:04:10.120
But it's so fast, we, you don't

00:04:10.120 --> 00:04:13.460
see it when you're watching.
You don't hear it.

00:04:13.460 --> 00:04:17.720
It does make you feel like you're
getting closer to something. Something

00:04:17.720 --> 00:04:22.020
real, like batting wings or something.

00:04:22.020 --> 00:04:23.990
And by using the interactions between

00:04:23.990 --> 00:04:29.540
ultrasound waves the display can even
produce audible sound.

00:04:29.540 --> 00:04:30.300
"Five"

00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:31.160
"Four"

00:04:31.160 --> 00:04:32.200
"Three"

00:04:32.200 --> 00:04:33.140
"Two"

00:04:33.140 --> 00:04:34.200
"One"

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:35.900
"Zero"

00:04:35.910 --> 00:04:41.070
At the moment the display can only create small, simple
shapes. But if the team can get their

00:04:41.070 --> 00:04:45.660
beads moving even faster, or can
manipulate multiple beads they could

00:04:45.660 --> 00:04:50.400
create bigger and more complex images.
And the team hopes that this could be

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:54.390
the start of 3D displays becoming part
of everyday life.

00:04:54.390 --> 00:04:59.250
The idea of just 3D content to be
something that's there. You go, you use it

00:04:59.250 --> 00:05:02.790
whenever you need to use it. You don't
need to use it, you don't, or I need to be

00:05:02.790 --> 00:05:07.440
seeing that 3D content but also my notes
or just check what time it is, you know.

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:14.610
Like blending that content in our
real day-to-day life just in a seamless

00:05:14.610 --> 00:05:16.820
manner it's something that's very
valuable.

00:05:16.820 --> 00:05:19.280
This is literally our first step.

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:24.419
We've got a long way to go but the
interesting bit is that the fundamentals

00:05:24.419 --> 00:05:30.600
that are underpinning this kind of
displays they are strong, they can really

00:05:30.600 --> 00:05:35.120
deliver all of this, and I think that's
very exciting.

